WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.597
This video follows a cable from the overhead
tray to its device.

2
00:00:07.347 --> 00:00:19.459
The rules come from DRS 01.00.03, Cable
Dressing Basket Trays, DRS 01.00.03.01, Cable

3
00:00:19.459 --> 00:00:31.714
Dressing Vertical Pathways, DRS 01.00.02,
Front of Rack Cable Dressing, and page seven

4
00:00:31.714 --> 00:00:33.566
of the guide.

5
00:00:34.916 --> 00:00:42.743
The general rules from lesson five still apply
the whole way: half-inch Velcro, never zip

6
00:00:42.743 --> 00:00:47.140
ties, bend radius, safe supports, and rated
cable.

7
00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.783
Start in the tray.

8
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:56.691
Cables run overhead in a basket tray.

9
00:00:57.441 --> 00:01:06.187
DRS 01.00.03, Cable Dressing Basket Trays, has
five rules for it.

10
00:01:07.587 --> 00:01:08.867
Rule one.

11
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:16.017
Bundle cables neatly in the tray with Velcro,
twelve to eighteen inches apart.

12
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:18.605
Rule two.

13
00:01:19.355 --> 00:01:22.939
Do not strap bundles to the tray on straight
runs.

14
00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:30.814
Secure them to the tray only going around
corners, at exits, and on vertical runs.

15
00:01:32.164 --> 00:01:36.580
At each corner, secure the bundle to the tray
three times.

16
00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.066
And at each exit, secure it three times.

17
00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:43.632
Rule three.

18
00:01:44.382 --> 00:01:47.881
Use half-inch Velcro for all cable bundling.

19
00:01:48.631 --> 00:01:57.612
That matches DRS 01.00.01 and 01.00.02.

20
00:01:58.962 --> 00:02:00.285
Rule four.

21
00:02:01.035 --> 00:02:06.091
Waterfalls are centered over the rack's
vertical cable managers.

22
00:02:06.841 --> 00:02:14.269
A waterfall is a curved guide on the tray edge
that keeps the bend wide where cable leaves

23
00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:15.011
the tray.

24
00:02:17.311 --> 00:02:18.570
Rule five.

25
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:25.272
Cables keep the manufacturer's recommended
bend radius at all times.

26
00:02:26.022 --> 00:02:31.227
The drawing marks twenty times the cable
diameter at a tray corner.

27
00:02:32.577 --> 00:02:42.455
That matches the twenty times minimum for
cable being pulled, from DRS 01.00.01.

28
00:02:43.205 --> 00:02:48.069
Keep tray corners at least that wide, and
confirm with your lead.

29
00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:53.109
At the exit, the waterfall is what keeps the
bend.

30
00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:58.619
Never let cable drop over a tray edge
unsupported.

31
00:02:59.369 --> 00:03:01.481
The tray wire would kink it.

32
00:03:05.631 --> 00:03:10.197
Some runs go up or down, in a vertical tray or
ladder.

33
00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:20.675
DRS 01.00.03.01, Cable Dressing Vertical
Pathways, covers them.

34
00:03:21.425 --> 00:03:26.630
Its number builds on 01.00.03.

35
00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.758
This is a side view.

36
00:03:30.508 --> 00:03:32.556
The right side is done right.

37
00:03:33.306 --> 00:03:34.586
Rule one.

38
00:03:35.336 --> 00:03:41.693
Bundle with Velcro every twelve to eighteen
inches, in vertical tray as well.

39
00:03:42.443 --> 00:03:45.942
On the horizontal runs the bundles float.

40
00:03:47.292 --> 00:03:48.529
Rule two.

41
00:03:49.279 --> 00:03:53.589
When the run goes vertical, secure the bundles
to the tray.

42
00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:56.155
Rule three.

43
00:03:56.905 --> 00:04:01.470
Vertical cable must be secured so it is
self-supporting.

44
00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:09.175
Too much cable weight on the horizontal
transitions damages the tray and the cable.

45
00:04:11.475 --> 00:04:12.797
Rule four.

46
00:04:13.547 --> 00:04:20.374
Waterfalls are required at all vertical
transitions, to keep the bend radius.

47
00:04:21.724 --> 00:04:22.983
Rule five.

48
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:29.706
Three-quarter inch Velcro may be used to
secure cable to the vertical tray.

49
00:04:30.456 --> 00:04:32.824
Bundling still uses half-inch.

50
00:04:34.174 --> 00:04:40.723
The drawing also says all bends are greater
than twenty times the cable diameter.

51
00:04:42.073 --> 00:04:44.271
Now the mistakes on the left.

52
00:04:45.021 --> 00:04:49.735
A missing waterfall at the transition can
damage the cable.

53
00:04:51.085 --> 00:04:58.104
And with too little Velcro, the cable slides
to the bottom under its own weight, damaging

54
00:04:58.104 --> 00:04:59.917
the cable and the tray.

55
00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:06.179
Next, the front of the rack.

56
00:05:06.929 --> 00:05:16.441
DRS 01.00.02, Front of Rack Cable Dressing,
covers how cable leaves the basket tray and

57
00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:20.049
comes down the front of the rack.

58
00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:22.729
Rule one.

59
00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:28.045
Cables shall not cross devices they do not
patch into.

60
00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:36.133
Every device must be replaceable without
unplugging cables that end in another device.

61
00:05:37.483 --> 00:05:38.721
Rule two.

62
00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:46.712
Cable leaves the basket tray directly above
the rack edge, or above the vertical cable

63
00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:47.385
manager.

64
00:05:48.735 --> 00:05:49.951
Rule three.

65
00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:58.178
Every cable entering or leaving the tray is
supported by a waterfall, to keep the bend

66
00:05:58.178 --> 00:05:58.787
radius.

67
00:05:59.537 --> 00:06:05.275
Here, too, the waterfall lines up with the
rack's vertical cable manager.

68
00:06:06.625 --> 00:06:07.948
Rule four.

69
00:06:08.698 --> 00:06:15.201
Network racks must use over-the-top
waterfalls, where the cable rides over the top

70
00:06:15.201 --> 00:06:16.549
edge of the tray.

71
00:06:17.899 --> 00:06:25.764
In this hall, the guide puts that note on the
DCN patch panel racks and the spine racks, in

72
00:06:25.764 --> 00:06:27.925
network rows one and two.

73
00:06:28.675 --> 00:06:32.750
Ask your lead which other racks count as
network racks.

74
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.359
Rule five.

75
00:06:36.109 --> 00:06:39.735
Bundle cable neatly with half-inch Velcro.

76
00:06:40.485 --> 00:06:49.616
That matches DRS 01.00.01 and 01.00.03.

77
00:06:50.966 --> 00:06:52.331
Rule six.

78
00:06:53.081 --> 00:06:57.988
Use the waterfall on the side of the rack the
cables patch into.

79
00:06:58.738 --> 00:07:03.389
These cables patch on the left, so they use
the left waterfall.

80
00:07:04.739 --> 00:07:12.582
And tray accessories, like waterfalls, must
fit the tray: trays made by Basor, WBT,

81
00:07:12.582 --> 00:07:20.141
Legrand, or equivalent, with two by four inch
openings and five millimeter wire.

82
00:07:20.891 --> 00:07:23.750
If one does not fit, stop and ask.

83
00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.636
One wording note.

84
00:07:27.386 --> 00:07:31.887
Rule six says should, while the other rules
say shall or must.

85
00:07:32.637 --> 00:07:39.869
Every DRS is a requirement, so treat it as
required, and confirm with your lead.

86
00:07:44.019 --> 00:07:47.539
The last rules come from page seven of the
guide.

87
00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:52.663
Their DRS sheet has not been added to this
course yet.

88
00:07:54.063 --> 00:08:00.975
In the vertical cable manager, cables come out
between the fingers closest to the device.

89
00:08:01.725 --> 00:08:06.183
Slack goes on the downstream side, toward the
end device.

90
00:08:06.933 --> 00:08:09.088
Nothing touches the floor.

91
00:08:10.438 --> 00:08:13.019
Tray tiers count from the bottom up.

92
00:08:13.769 --> 00:08:16.991
Tier one carries the OCS cables.

93
00:08:17.741 --> 00:08:25.378
Tier two carries the 144-fiber trunks and, at
this site, the CAT6 copper.

94
00:08:26.728 --> 00:08:28.840
One conflict in the guide.

95
00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:33.707
Page fourteen still says copper goes in tier
one.

96
00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:38.767
This course teaches tier two, but confirm with
your lead.

97
00:08:40.117 --> 00:08:44.874
And trunk slack is stored on top of the rack,
never in the tray.

98
00:08:49.024 --> 00:08:51.733
Four points to confirm with your lead.

99
00:08:52.733 --> 00:08:55.421
Which racks count as network racks.

100
00:08:56.171 --> 00:09:03.559
The DRS does not list them, so ask whether end
of row and SPOCS racks need over-the-top

101
00:09:03.559 --> 00:09:04.833
waterfalls too.

102
00:09:06.183 --> 00:09:13.372
Rule six of DRS 01.00.02 says should, not
shall.

103
00:09:14.122 --> 00:09:16.789
This course teaches it as required.

104
00:09:18.139 --> 00:09:20.251
Bends in the tray pathway.

105
00:09:21.001 --> 00:09:28.216
The basket tray and vertical pathway drawings
show more than twenty times the diameter,

106
00:09:28.216 --> 00:09:34.270
while the general sheet allows ten times once
cable is not under tension.

107
00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:39.351
This course teaches more than twenty times in
tray pathways.

108
00:09:40.701 --> 00:09:43.261
And the copper tray tier from the guide.

109
00:09:47.411 --> 00:09:53.832
Let us follow one bundle from the tray to a
device, and name the rule at each step.

110
00:09:54.832 --> 00:10:01.829
First, check that the rating on the jacket
matches every space the run passes through.

111
00:10:02.579 --> 00:10:08.531
That is DRS 01.00.01, rule six.

112
00:10:09.881 --> 00:10:16.706
In the tray, bundle it with half-inch Velcro
every twelve to eighteen inches, loose on

113
00:10:16.706 --> 00:10:17.817
straight runs.

114
00:10:18.567 --> 00:10:24.605
DRS 01.00.03, rules one to three.

115
00:10:25.955 --> 00:10:32.618
At the corner, keep the bend at least twenty
times the diameter, and secure it to the tray

116
00:10:32.618 --> 00:10:33.507
three times.

117
00:10:34.257 --> 00:10:36.027
Rules two and five.

118
00:10:37.377 --> 00:10:44.097
At the exit, it goes over the waterfall
centered over the vertical cable manager, on

119
00:10:44.097 --> 00:10:48.577
the side it patches into, and it is secured
three times.

120
00:10:49.327 --> 00:10:58.714
That is DRS 01.00.03 and DRS 01.00.02.

121
00:11:00.064 --> 00:11:06.214
If the route climbs a vertical tray, the
bundle is secured to the tray so it is

122
00:11:06.214 --> 00:11:10.261
self-supporting, with waterfalls at the
transitions.

123
00:11:11.011 --> 00:11:16.942
That is DRS 01.00.03.01.

124
00:11:18.292 --> 00:11:23.177
Down the rack, it never crosses a device it
does not patch into.

125
00:11:23.927 --> 00:11:29.005
DRS 01.00.02, rule one.

126
00:11:30.355 --> 00:11:36.904
In the vertical cable manager, it exits
between the fingers closest to the device.

127
00:11:37.654 --> 00:11:40.299
Slack goes on the downstream side.

128
00:11:41.049 --> 00:11:43.353
And nothing touches the floor.

129
00:11:47.503 --> 00:11:54.938
In lesson 5B of the course, explore the basket
tray, a vertical pathway and the front of the

130
00:11:54.938 --> 00:12:02.373
rack, put the steps in order, match each rule
to its source, and spot the dressing mistakes.

131
00:12:03.123 --> 00:12:05.213
Then take the lesson check.

